A||||12||PlasticWrapd||Don't use orange wire to clear computer!!!||||4||
Z||000000||PlasticWrapd||11-30-2000||11:57 AM||||The online service guide mentions clearing the stored codes in the computer's memory by disconnecting the power wire to the computer, AKA the orange wire at the junction block.  DON'T DO IT!!!!  I used this method to clear the computer and ended up toasting the PROM chip, twice!  The ONLY safe method to clear the computer is disconnecting the *NEGATIVE* battery terminal.  The computer operates all of it's "electronic switches" by switching the ground (or negative terminal) on and off.  Since all of these "switches" are protected by diodes, so voltage spikes from static electricity cannot reach the delicate components inside the ECM.  Connecting positive terminal or orange wire while the system is connected to ground can allow static electricity to travel through the ECM and destroy it.  Protect the ECM and use the battery ground to clear the memory.||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||11-30-2000||08:36 PM||||If you are blowing the PROM by clearing codes with the orange wire then something is seriously wrong with your ECM. The orange wire was put there by GM specifically for this purpose. It's so service people can clears the ECM without wiping the clock and radio presets. It sound like a small thing but its something that Really pisses off customers.<P>The ECM is static sensitive but you have some of your information wrong. The ECM diodes you are refering to are to disipate feedback current from the solinoids they control. They don't do anything for static.<P>The ECM actually has MOV devices on lines GM was worried about static and other transients on. MOVs, short for Metal Oxide Varistors, are the devices used in most computer surge control devices.<P>Also keep in mind the ECM does not run on battery voltage. It has it's own internal regulators that run all the chips. There are only a few lines exposed to battery voltage and, with the exception of power inputs, they are all line drivers transistors or MOSFETs. The TPS, MAP, Coolant Temp, and MAT  sensors are all 5v driven. (O2 generates its own voltage, as does the crank position sensor on DIS.) ||||reg||1
Z||000002||PlasticWrapd||12-01-2000||12:48 AM||||Actually, it was a tech over at the Pontiac dealership that told me not to use the orange wire to clear the computer.  He told me that he refuses to clear the computer using this method as he has had many instances where everthig was fine until he tried to clear the codes this way.  He also told me he has seen other techs toast the ECM this way.  Rather than take the chance of toasting the ECM, I'll stick with the negative terminal method.  resetting a clock and a few radio stations is a small price to pay when you consider the cost of a replacement ECM.  <P>Perhaps I misunderstood the actual diode explanation, but the tech made it quite clear about disconnecting the negative battery terminal.  Could a static discharge, while disconnecting the orange wire, cause problems with the ECM?  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-01-2000||01:20 AM||||The other way is to pull the fuse for the ECM from the fuse block for one minute and put it back in. Lots less hassle than disconnecting the battery.||||reg||1
Z||000004||prof bobo||12-01-2000||11:33 AM||||I have to agree with Ogre, his description of the ECM circuitry is spot on.  As a matter of fact I'm looking at a circuit board from an ECM in front of me right now.<P>I see 13 diodes and perhaps 4 MOVs.  That the ECM operates switches by closing them to ground.  Well, yeah it might do that, it's called a 'sinking' output (allows current to flow into to it, probably to ground).  But it also has 'sourcing' outputs, which supply a current to something.  Ogre's right about some of the diodes absorbing 'feedback' current.  Technically it's called reverse EMF, and it happens anytime you energize a coil of wire and then remove the energy source -it delivers a reverse current back to the source and most transistors don't like that, hence diodes to ground to dump it.  Some of the diodes on input look like probably Zener diodes and are likely there to prevent admitting a voltage which would exceed that of the input range of the device ('clamps' the voltage at some limiting value).  And yep, the MOV's absorb spikes and noise -don't forget that everytime they act their lifetime becomes shortened just a bit, which is why computer surge suppressors 'wear out'.  <P>The ECM appears to be pretty rugged, the designers must have recognized the hostile environment in which it was targeted to operate; most of the inputs appear filtered, clamped or buffered in some way and most of the outputs are through drivers of some sort.<BR>I'd like to know what these parts are, who makes 'em so I could look up data sheets on 'em, but I'd bet that most of them have protective circuitry (it's so common) which would let them sense excessive current draw and shut down to prevent damage -i.e. you could probably short outputs to ground, +12 or each other and not toast the ECM.<P>Yeah, static can be a problem for electronic components, but I don't think its a big consideration at the ECM connectors. Now, if you are handling the board or the PROM it's a different story.  I'd guess that unless you are generating so much static that you are seeing arcing sparks to that orange connector, your problems are of a very different nature.<P>And don't put to much faith in the words of a repair tech from GM with respect to design issues involving ECMs.  There is a difference between technicians and engineers.  Just because he's seen a pattern of failures, it doesn't mean he's necessarily defined a mode of failure.  It may be coincidence, and it could be due to his own faulty procedures.<BR>I've seen GM techs do a fair amount of replacing this part and that part (in my industry we call it 'board swapping') until a problem appears to go away ... only to return later and not get fixed until someone actually properly diagnoses it, finds and reveals through testing a faulty part before replacing it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||12-01-2000||12:40 PM||||i've had a switch on these SAME 2 wires for over 2 years using it at LEAST once a week some times 4-5 times a night (at the track) and i've never had a problem with it. (nitrous/spark knock would cause it to default to safe mode or limp home mode clearing the computer would drop that mode and i could run nitrous safely again.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-01-2000||09:06 PM||||I spent considerable time today looking at the power regulators in a C3 and a P4 ECM. Basically if the ECM is blowing PROMs you've got some kind of major problem with the ECM internal power regulator. There's no way that kind of energy should be able to reach the PROM or the Processor.<P>I don't put much into what most dealer or other mechanics say. There are very bloody few really good ones out there. Many mechanics still have little or no real grasp of the ECM/PCM systems. A frighteningly large number of them don't even have proper tools to work on the computers. They just assume if they can't find an obvious sensor dead it must be the ECM/PCM box.<P>Prof BoBo, you can find most all of the GM C3 and P4 class ECM schematics at <A HREF="http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/" TARGET=_blank>Ludis' GMECM site</A>. He's done extensive work on reverse engineering them. Do note tho that some of the terminal names he uses on teh external connectors don't exactly match GM published documents. (Yes there are diodes Zeniers MOSFETS transistors and some other things on the inputs and outputs.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||prof bobo||12-04-2000||12:11 PM||||Ogre:  Thanks for the info, BTW what's your level of expertise/background with this kind of stuff? <P>You know I think I've run across that site that site, along with some others -I seem to recall a group that's got a DIY ECM/fuel injection project going.<P>When I first started playing around with these things it was with an idea to build a test jig for problem determination.  I keep meaning to build 'scan tool' to read the serial data into a laptop. As the years have gone by, my experience has been that I've never actually seen an ECM failure, it's almost always a mechanical problem, corroded connectors, bad wires or failed sensor.  It's a ruggedized reliable unit, if it fails then it's either early on due to a marginal component and warranty has caught most of these, or because somebody did something very nasty to it.<P>My latest idea is to get one of these evaluation units from Z-World (http://zworld@zworld.com) that has a color LCD, touch screen, digital and analog inputs and write some code to both replace my dashboard components AND include a dedicated onboard scan tool.  <P>But I still got a lot to do with the bare frame in my garage first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ...||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-04-2000||07:53 PM||||Because I have an 87 with DIS that's basically ignored in the small aftermarket books, I've had to learn allot more about GM ECMs than I ever really wanted to.<P>The link you are thinking of is <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A> <P>I have the AutoXray GM scanner. It's a GM 84-95 only scan tool. It was about 150$us. It's a few buttons and a 2 line LCD to read everything. I'm not sure if AutoXray still makes it. This one is a "subset" of their XP240 universal scanner.<P>There are 3 kinds of ECM fails common. One is a cracked off component on the board. Heat cycling and marginal construction cause that one. Some older ECM are more prone to it than the newer ones. <P>The other fail is often caused by someone or something shorting the 5 volt regulator out in the ECM. Most of the sensors run on 5 volts or less. If you connect battery voltage to them or short them to ground then poof goes the ECM.<P>A third fail is on the ECM's that have pin terminals for the plugs. The pin terminals can crack loose from the main board. Allot of GM ECM's use edge connectors both for cost and to avoid these cracked terminal joints. the latest ECMs use pin termials but they are much better built than the old ones were.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||prof bobo||12-04-2000||11:31 PM||||Ogre:  Out of 100 problems that smell ECM related, what % turned out to be a failed ECM?  I'd bet not many.<P>How many times have you heard someone say they took their car to the dealer and paid $$$ to get the 'computer' replaced and, THEN, after that they had to replace another part, some sensor?  Scary, huh?  And people wonder why I insist on working on my own cars ...<P>Anyway, with regard to your 3 modes of failure, I nod yes to 1 & 3 but 2 don't sound right to me.  I might have to try sacrificing one of my spare boards to test your theory.  I say that the 5v the ECM delivers to sensors is current limited, the engineers should have anticipated shorts to ground, and even then, it's been a long long time since I've seen an integrated circuit, packaged regulator that didn't have built-in overcurrent detection and auto shut-down.  12v to a 5v input ought to have a zener clamp, the engineers should have also anticipated shorts to 12v.  However shorts to a negative voltage could be _bad_, but there's not much of that under the hood.  But what about leakage from spark plug wiring?  Hmm, multi Kv into a 5v input -well that's probably going to leap around all the internal circuitry and always be pretty devastating.  <P>One thing that's sure to toast the ECM is overvoltage on the 12v supply side -a failed alternator regulator running the charging system hot ... the 5v regulators I'm used have an input voltage limit at around 35v -exceed that and most of them magically transform into low ohms value resistors, and that spells doom to whatever they feed.<P>On last thing that a lot of folks miss is the wierd things that can happen when their negative battery cable connections become corroded.  When you go to crank that puppy and the starter tries to suck that hundred amps or more out of the battery, that current _has_ to find a way to ground.  If you ain't got a good negative cable contact, then it's going to try to divide it through every OTHER wire leading to ground.  Ever notice how many ground references there are leading to the ECM?  Scary huh . . .||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||12-04-2000||11:44 PM||||No, I don't beleive many fail without help from some doink at a dealer or some similar thing.<P>I'm not sure how rugged the power supply is in these. It will likely take some abuse on the sensor power leads but not allot. I'm still reading over Ludis' schematics. It's allot slower going when I don't have a big printer or a ploter to run them out on. The GIF's don't reduce well.<P>As for charging system issues.... did you see the tip on erratic idle I posted. That is just one of the charging system issues I've seen confirmation of doing weird things.<P>Another one that's even more anoying to solve is current loops and voltage drops on crappy ground wires. Grounds that may look good often arent. A ground with more than a 1 volt drop across it can do all sorts of weird things to the car.||||reg||1
Z||000011||prof bobo||12-05-2000||11:49 AM||||Those schematics are somewhat useful, but without data sheets for the proprietary parts, well, about all you can do is make assumptions about what they do and speculate about min/max ratings.  <P>Well why don't I just take quick look at the your schematics ...<P>Hmm,  CR3 is obviously trying to ensure that reversing power supply polarity to the ECM is not fatal.  RV1 is shunting input transients to ground, CR2 is guarding against overvoltage (runaway alternator) -it'd be nice to know component types so I could figure values and ratings.  U1 is a strange looking beast, evidently where we're getting 5v.  Is it a switching regulator?  Looks like it can sense current draw, assuming the 'I' in the VCCxISENSE inputs is for current, looks like current through Q2 could be related to the voltage that would appear across R2. But dunno if that's for protective circuitry in U1, or for just plain current regulation in addition to voltage regulation.  But anyway that's VCC -5v onboard primary supply.  WHT_VCC looks like 5v feed to sensors, simpler regulation -I wonder how C10 comes in to play with  VCC2ISENSE, perhaps its just transient current regulation to stabilize readings from the sensors.  Hmm, do ya suppose RT1 is all the protection there is for shorts of 5v sensor feed to ground?  I'd bet 'ol U1 wouldn't be amused much if you shorted the 5v sensor feed output to 12v -that could be ... bad.<P>I'd guess the RESET input is our 'orange wire', presumably it kills the standby current to the RAM containing stored codes and learned values.<P>Unfortunately, without access to replacements for the proprietary parts, ECM's are largely unrepairable.  However, if I wasn't getting 5v sensor feed, RT1 and Q3 would be likely failure points and maybe a fixable thing.<P>But maybe someone who knows more than me can tell me why I couldn't replace the whole mess with some 7805's and be done with it.<P>I'll just keep a cupla spare boards around and know better than to do 'doinky' things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||12-05-2000||11:45 PM||||Allot of the part pinouts are available for the chips. Acutally most of the parts in there aren't proprietary. Most of them are off the shelf chips with GM numbers. So far only the power chip is one I don't for sure know what it is.<P>The values for the MOVs diodes Zeniers and MOSFETs isn't that hard to work out. The math for most of the circuits is very easy to come by. You'd be supirsed the amount of data online for these ECM's. The main thing stopping most people from fixing them is the lack of tools for handling suface mount parts.<P>GM pretty much avoided custom parts in the ECMs until about 93-95 when OBD2 and all that cranked up. It costs money to generate custom logic. There really is no justifiable cost to do so for the C3 and P4 ECM.||||reg||1
